          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "Yes, on one point they do, but the main message is consistent.\n\n**The consistent position:** The asthma guidance in the \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy\" document (a global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel summary) says that treating asthma with a short-acting reliever alone is no longer appropriate at any severity. It explains that reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is linked to severe flare-ups, even when symptoms seem infrequent. Heavy reliever use is also described as a risk marker in its own right.\n\n**Where the guidance differs:** The same document says the preferred reliever for mild asthma is a point of disagreement. Global strategy guidance favours an inhaled corticosteroid combined with formoterol as the reliever across steps. Some national panel guidance says a short-acting bronchodilator reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate in mild disease. Both positions still include a regular controller; they differ on which reliever to use.\n\n**What the results did not cover:** The results did not give details of the reasons behind the national panel's position, or say how common reliever-only use is. They also do not cover what to do about your own treatment. If you use a reliever and wonder whether your plan fits this guidance, bring that question to your clinician or asthma nurse, and ask for a check of your inhaler technique at the same time.\n\nIf you are having breathing difficulty right now, or your reliever is no longer relieving symptoms, seek urgent medical care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
